WEBVTT

0
00:02.180 --> 00:08.990
First of all, we will briefly discuss the evolution of the mobile communication systems from 1G to

1
00:08.990 --> 00:14.380
the 5G, you might ask the question that what does this G stands for?

2
00:14.420 --> 00:16.540
This G stands for Generation.

3
00:16.880 --> 00:25.730
That means that 1 G stands for first generation of the mobile communication systems and the most famous

4
00:25.730 --> 00:33.370
1G systems were AMPS and NMTS and these 1G systems were analog systems.

5
00:33.380 --> 00:40.790
That means that they did not provide any data services, they only provided analog voice services.

6
00:41.120 --> 00:47.420
So we moved on from 1G systems to the 2G systems and among the 2G systems...

7
00:47.420 --> 00:55.970
GSM is the most famous and the most widely deployed  2G system that is still deployed in many parts

8
00:55.970 --> 00:57.150
of the world.

9
00:57.590 --> 01:08.480
And when the GPRS was added on top of the GSM technology, then this 2G systems became

10
01:08.480 --> 01:12.440
2.5G systems and in the 2.5G systems...

11
01:12.770 --> 01:20.790
A user experienced a data rate of 50 kbps under the normal circumstances.

12
01:21.530 --> 01:29.540
So then we moved on from the 2.5 G systems to the 2.75G  systems as the demand

13
01:29.540 --> 01:32.420
for the data services kept on rising.

14
01:33.050 --> 01:43.520
And when we added the EDGE technology on top of the GSM technology, we got the 2.75G

15
01:43.530 --> 01:49.700
systems in which user experienced a data rate of 150 kilobits per second.

16
01:51.030 --> 01:58.260
And then there was a fundamental shift in the technology, we moved from the 2G systems to the 3G systems

17
01:58.650 --> 02:06.660
and in the 3G systems, UMTS is the most widely deployed and the most famous 3G technology in which

18
02:06.670 --> 02:13.950
user experienced a data rate of 384 kilobits per second under the normal conditions.

19
02:14.670 --> 02:24.330
And then we moved on to the 3.5 G systems in which HSPA was added on top of the UMTS

20
02:24.330 --> 02:25.090
technology.

21
02:25.560 --> 02:34.080
And in this case, user experienced a data rate of 10 megabits per second, and then we moved from

22
02:34.080 --> 02:42.360
3.5 to 3.75 G systems in which the HSPA+ technology was used in order

23
02:42.360 --> 02:44.390
to provide the data services.

24
02:44.850 --> 02:51.520
And in these systems, the users experienced a data rate of 20 megabits per second.

25
02:52.740 --> 02:53.790
.

26
02:53.790 --> 03:00.000
Then there was a fundamental shift in the technology and we moved from the 3G systems to the 4G systems.

27
03:00.510 --> 03:10.260
And LTE is a 4G system in which a user experienced a data rate of 20 to 50 megabits per second depending

28
03:10.260 --> 03:12.210
upon the channel conditions.

29
03:13.470 --> 03:21.630
And then we moved from LTE to the LTE advanced in which the targeted data rate was one gigabits per

30
03:21.900 --> 03:23.730
second per user.

31
03:24.180 --> 03:31.440
But the actual data that the user experienced, it was in low hundreds of megabits per second.

32
03:31.950 --> 03:40.110
And then we moved on from the 4G technology to 5G technology in which the targeted data rate is 20 gigabits

33
03:40.110 --> 03:40.860
per second.

34
03:41.190 --> 03:48.460
But the actual data that a user experiences it is in high hundreds of megabits per second.

35
03:48.870 --> 03:56.070
So here we can see that the fundamental driver in the evolution of the mobile communication system is

36
03:56.070 --> 04:01.530
the requirement to provide higher and higher data to the user.